          loading="lazy" 
        />
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section class="section" id="manufacturing-process">
        <h2>Liquid Detergent Manufacturing Process – Step by Step</h2>
        <p>
          At an industrial scale, manufacturing a liquid detergent is an active, kinetically controlled chemical process, absolutely not a passive fluid transfer operation. The intensity of mechanical mixing shear, the precise chronological sequence of raw material addition, and the strict control of exothermic heat directly dictate the success of micelle formation and the ultimate physical stability of the finished product.
        </p>
        <p>
          Inadequate mechanical agitation during the addition of highly viscous active surfactants results in localized zones of extreme concentration, leading to "fisheyes" and un-dissolved gel lumps. Conversely, applying excessive high-shear mixing can irreparably rupture the fragile rod-like micellar structures responsible for the product's thick, luxurious viscosity. Consequently, commercial manufacturing demands rigorously engineered standard operating procedures:
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
          <li><strong>Water Conditioning</strong>: The bulk deionized water phase must be properly charged and treated with chelating agents to establish a controlled, mineral-free dilution environment.</li>
          <li><strong>Sequential Surfactant Addition</strong>: Active ingredients must be introduced in a specific sequence (typically anionic, then nonionic, then amphoteric) to prevent localized over-concentration and catastrophic gel blocking.</li>
          <li><strong>Exothermic Neutralization</strong>: Strong acid precursors (like LABSA) must be neutralized with alkaline agents (like Caustic Soda) under carefully monitored cooling to prevent thermal degradation of the polymer network.</li>
          <li><strong>Electrolyte Viscosity Tuning</strong>: Precise, fractional additions of sodium chloride are utilized to manipulate the electrical double layer of the micelles, forcing them to elongate and dynamically build fluid viscosity.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Image: Phase Separation -->
      <div class="image-slot">
        <img 
          src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/AGBzPMBJDQiwDGny/liquid-detergent-phase-separation-instability-qiS98De8QS0HT0Dd.png" 
