      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section class="section" id="failure-modes">
        <h2>Poorly Formulated Liquid Detergents: Chemical Consequences</h2>
        <p>
          When liquid detergents fail, they do so for highly predictable, fundamentally chemical reasons rather than due to random manufacturing defects. Tragically, many of these profound thermodynamic failures only manifest weeks or months after the product has left the factory, emerging during retail distribution, extreme seasonal temperature swings, or after the consumer opens the bottle for the first time.
        </p>
        <p>
          From a strict physical chemistry standpoint, instability inevitably arises the moment absolute solubility limits are breached, when the chemical preservative network is biologically overwhelmed, or when the pH buffers fail, causing the system to drift outside its safe stability window. For a startup brand, launching a product with latent instability is commercial suicide, leading to massive retail returns and irreversible brand damage:
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
          <li><strong>Catastrophic Phase Separation</strong>: Occurs when thermodynamic solubility limits are exceeded, causing the fluid to physically split into two distinct, unusable liquid layers.</li>
          <li><strong>Fragrance Dropout</strong>: Caused by a lack of sufficient hydrotropes, resulting in the hydrophobic perfume oils separating and floating to the top, leaving the bulk fluid smelling chemical and raw.</li>
          <li><strong>Viscosity Collapse</strong>: Triggered by an electrolyte imbalance or extreme thermal stress, suddenly transforming a premium thick gel into a watery, unappealing liquid.</li>
          <li><strong>Microbial Rancidity</strong>: A result of weak or incompatible chemical preservation, leading to the rapid proliferation of dangerous bacteria, putrid odors, and severe consumer health risks.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6: FAQ -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
