            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. Is liquid detergent formulation the same for all applications?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              No. Hand dishwashing liquids, heavy-duty laundry liquids, and institutional floor cleaners require drastically different surfactant architectures, varying foam stability profiles, and specific alkalinity buffers tailored exactly to their unique end-use environments.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. Can a lab formulation be scaled directly?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Direct mathematical scaling almost universally fails. This is due to massive differences in mechanical mixing shear energy, significantly reduced heat transfer efficiency, and the dangerous localized concentration gradients that inevitably occur inside massive industrial mixing tanks.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. Why do liquid detergents separate over time?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Phase separation is an irreversible thermodynamic failure. It typically results from severe surfactant incompatibility (such as improperly mixing anionic and cationic species), an excessive electrolyte imbalance, pushing fragrance solubility past its limit, or severe formulation pH drift.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Call to Action Box (target="_top") -->
